Implement every callable directly from its Cott contract with deterministic standard-library Python.
Do not copy, translate, quote, inspect, import, or reuse upstream Harlequin source or prose.
Use only Python standard-library modules (including sqlite3), generated Cott facade/type modules, and cott_runtime; do not add dependencies or bindings.
NEVER use subprocess, multiprocessing, shell commands, external executables, dynamic imports, or any mechanism that launches a child process (including os.system, os.popen, os.spawn*, or asyncio subprocess APIs).
Split semicolons only outside SQL strings, quoted identifiers, and line or block comments.
Reject empty SQL and unterminated strings, identifiers, or block comments with SqlClientError variants.
Execute split statements in order and return one QueryResult per statement.
In read-only mode use SQLite query_only enforcement and map denied mutations to ReadOnlyViolation.
Reject mutations hidden in a WITH statement as well as direct write statements.
For `DatabaseTarget_Memory`, use the string `":memory:"`. For `DatabaseTarget_File`, pass `target.path` directly to `sqlite3.connect`, but any helper annotated to return `str` for a database name MUST return `str(target.path)`, never `target.path`; this prevents Path-to-str return errors.
Return cells as Null, Integer, Real, Text, or Blob; reject other SQLite values as UnsupportedValue.
List catalog tables and views from main.sqlite_master excluding names beginning sqlite_.
Search relation and column names using case-insensitive literal substring matching.
Order catalog relations by name, columns by ordinal, and search results by relation then kind then ordinal.
Render cells as NULL, decimal integers, Python repr-style finite floats, text with \\n, \\r, and \\t escaped, and blobs as lowercase 0x hex.
Render tables and catalog output as ASCII grids with deterministic column widths and left-aligned cells.

Use CottList by its public ABI: iterate it directly (`for item in items`) and convert it with `list(items)` or `tuple(items)`. NEVER access `items.values`; BasedPyright's missing-attribute error for `.values` is forbidden.
Construct CottList only as `CottList(values=tuple(...))`, including empty lists as `CottList(values=())`.
For an empty CottList with a known element type, bind a concrete annotation first (for example, `rows: CottList[Row] = CottList(values=())`); do not rely on empty-literal inference or casts.
Preserve concrete element types through loops: annotate empty CottList accumulators and keep loop variables/derived collections typed rather than allowing Unknown propagation.
